\chapter{Value loops and result joins}
\label{ch:18}
\index{Value loops and result joins}
\index{Control flow!value joins}

A value loop permits \OnxCodeTerm{break with value} and a labeled spelling for an enclosing value loop. The break operands constrain a common result type. An exit to an outer labeled loop can cross inner range and match scopes. Exactly the selected result flows out, while checked lexical cleanup still runs for scopes crossed by the exit.

\OnxInputCode[firstline=2,lastline=20]{../../on1x/examples/value_loops.on1x}
The implementation lowers the source into canonical regions, exits, and result slots. The verifier checks that a reachable result is initialized before read and that iterations cannot carry an illegally initialized slot around a backedge. The example finds the first square above a threshold and uses a typed fallback when the search is exhausted. Exercise the early exit and the fallback. Then give the fallback an incompatible type to see how the checker locates a failed join. \cref{ch:78} follows the same verification machinery for matches.

\section{Follow the value as well as the destination}
Take limits 7 and 8 in the square search. For 7 the last candidate
is 6. Its square is 36, so control goes through the empty match arm
and then out of the exhausted range. The next break supplies minus
one to the expression loop. For 8 the range includes 7. Its square
49 binds the pattern variable and satisfies the guard. That arm sends
49 straight to the exit of the labeled expression loop. It does not
resume after the range and overwrite 49 with minus one. These two
adjacent inputs distinguish the end convention of the range as well
as the target of the break.

Think of the result as a slot with two possible writers but only one
write on any path to its read. Iterations that do not yet have an
answer should leave it uninitialized. A continue is one such path;
it cannot stand for a default zero result. For an additional exercise,
look at the tuple-valued loop in the same file. Its successful exit
publishes one pair, so the flag and the printed number come from the
same iteration. Were a second exit to supply a number alone, the
caller could not destructure the joined result into the two requested
leaves. The checker must reject that mismatch before there is a slot
layout to emit.

